Program 5: Pie Chart, 3D Pie Chart and Bar Chart in R
Aim: To write an R program to draw (i) Pie Chart, (ii) 3D Pie Chart, and (iii) Bar Chart along with chart legend by considering a suitable CSV file.
Step 1: Write the complete R program
Write this program in your lab exam. I have added comments to make each step easy to understand.

# Program 5: Pie Chart, 3D Pie Chart and Bar Chart

# Install and load the package
install.packages("plotrix")
library(plotrix)

# Create data
Country <- c("India", "America", "Japan", "Australia")
Population <- c(40, 25, 20, 15)

# Create a data frame
data <- data.frame(Country, Population)

# Store data in CSV file
write.csv(data, "country.csv", row.names = FALSE)

# Read the CSV file
data <- read.csv("country.csv")

# Display the data
print(data)

# 1. Pie Chart
pie(data$Population,
    labels = data$Country,
    main = "Pie Chart",
    col = rainbow(4))

# 2. 3D Pie Chart
pie3D(data$Population,
      labels = data$Country,
      main = "3D Pie Chart",
      col = rainbow(4))

# 3. Bar Chart with Legend
barplot(data$Population,
        names.arg = data$Country,
        main = "Country Population",
        xlab = "Countries",
        ylab = "Population",
        col = rainbow(4))

legend("topright",
       legend = data$Country,
       fill = rainbow(4))



Important: install.packages("plotrix") is needed only the first time. If the package is already installed, you can skip that line. You need an internet connection to install it.
Step 2: Understand the program line by line
1. Install and load the package
install.packages("plotrix")
library(plotrix)


- install.packages("plotrix") installs the package required for the 3D pie chart.
- library(plotrix) loads the package into R.
2. Create the data
Country <- c("India", "America", "Japan", "Australia")
Population <- c(40, 25, 20, 15)


- Country stores the country names.
- Population stores the corresponding values.
- c() combines values into a vector.
3. Create a data frame
data <- data.frame(Country, Population)


This combines the country names and population values into a table.
4. Create and read the CSV file
write.csv(data, "country.csv", row.names = FALSE)
data <- read.csv("country.csv")
print(data)


- write.csv() saves the data into a CSV file.
- row.names = FALSE prevents extra row names from being saved.
- read.csv() reads the data from the CSV file.
- print() displays the data.
Step 3: Draw the three charts
1. Pie Chart
pie(data$Population,
    labels = data$Country,
    main = "Pie Chart",
    col = rainbow(4))


- pie() draws a pie chart.
- data$Population gives the values.
- labels displays country names.
- main gives the chart title.
- rainbow(4) provides four different colors.
2. 3D Pie Chart
pie3D(data$Population,
      labels = data$Country,
      main = "3D Pie Chart",
      col = rainbow(4))


- pie3D() draws a three-dimensional pie chart.
- The remaining arguments specify labels, title, and colors.
3. Bar Chart with Legend
barplot(data$Population,
        names.arg = data$Country,
        main = "Country Population",
        xlab = "Countries",
        ylab = "Population",
        col = rainbow(4))

legend("topright",
       legend = data$Country,
       fill = rainbow(4))


- barplot() draws the bar chart.
- names.arg displays country names below the bars.
- xlab and ylab give the axis labels.
- legend() displays the chart legend at the top-right.
- fill assigns the legend colors.
